## Deliberately not taken
- `src/player/vault.js` — the client half of the upstream project's encrypted-audio service (ECDH to
  their key server, AES-GCM parts of their song). It cannot run without their server, and using it
  against them would be circumventing their access control, which the AGPL does not grant. This kit
  plays a local audio file instead.
- The numbers in `src/player/cost.json.js` — a per-shot frame-cost table measured on the upstream
  author's own machine (`Apple M5 · Chrome/154.0.8037.95`) for their own film. Only the *mechanism*
  is reproduced here, fed by `scripts/calibrate.mjs` measuring each project on its own.

## Reference only (no code taken)

### `mexicat/pdoom-video` — https://github.com/mexicat/pdoom-video

A second film engine, and a closer sibling than the first: a generative, code-rendered music video
where every frame is a deterministic function of song time, previewed live in a browser and exported
frame-for-frame through ffmpeg. Copyright (c) 2026 Giacomo Magnanini, MIT licence.

Nothing from it is copied into this repository. What was taken is method, and it is credited where it
is used: `references/treatment.md` follows the shape of its `docs/TREATMENT.md` (idea, tone, palette
per act, the voice each face speaks, and a plate table of `id | window | what it says`), and the six
type rules in `references/craft.md` — measured word gaps, no haloed type, typographic punctuation
except in the instrument voice, no kerning across runs, frame-index-seeded noise, one headline at a
time — are its written rules, restated with the failures we measured ourselves. Its motion-blur
sampler (`--samples auto`, per-tile convergence) and its word-level lyric alignment (Demucs, CTC
forced alignment, Whisper) are documented here as the techniques a future revision should reach for;
neither is implemented in this kit.


Everything not listed above is original work. The engine's architecture — shots as data, absolute
song time, joins declared on the incoming shot, text layers composited before and after grading,
analysis-driven key functions — was studied in the same upstream project without copying its code.
Every file of upstream `src/player/` is now accounted for above: ported, ported-and-changed, or
deliberately not taken. `src/engine/` and `src/ch/` remain reference only — studied, not copied — and
where anything further is ported it gets an entry here first.
A read-only checkout for reference may be placed at `third_party/world-execute-web/`; it is not
committed and is not part of this work's source.

